.! --------------
.!
.vs progname "oct"
.vs product "Objective C Translator"
.!
.source manual.rinc
.!
.! --------------
.!
.header /OCT(OOPS)//${vers}/
.footer /%//Page #/
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Name
${progname} - Invokes Object Oriented C Translator
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Syntax
.bold
${progname}
[options].. method__file
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Description
.!
.program ${progname}
is an ${product} translator.
It provides Smalltalk-like message expressions to the "C" programmer.
The system does not provide binary operator overloading, ala Ada and C++.
However, since the operators are named, these methods are also
overloadable.
It accepts a superset of "C" and outputs "C" code acceptable to
.program cc,
which the user must invoke.
The translator uses interface header files for
references generated by message expressions.
.sp
.!
The input filename is an ${product} source language file.
The source file must have an extension of
.filename _.m
_.
The input file must include the header file
.filename <objc/objc.h>
(or an @includes() directive)
sothat the translated
.filename _.c
file can be compiled correctly.
If the translator approves of the syntax in the
.filename _.m
file, then a
.filename _.c
file will be produced.
If a file translates successfully,
.program cc
may still complain of errors.
Lines numbers will refer back to the original
.filename _.m
file.
.!* The translator tries not to rely on too much
.!* from the host operating system.
.!* The ability to have the shell startup a program named
.!* .program cpp
.!* is all that is needed.
The user invokes the C compiler manually.
.!* A prelinker examines the "C" file, not object files,
.!* for external class references,
.!* generating a list of references that
.!* must be compiled by the user, also.
.!
.!
  The translator should be used on files defining a new class,
and on files containing message expressions.
Both types of files are outlined below.
The file containing main() must be translated,
as main() is changed to appl__main()
so the ${product} runtime package can get a chance
to initialize itself.
The translator allows definition of classes with extend the
known types of the base system.
Single inheritence from another class is provided.
An additonal comment style like BCPL and C++ of "//" indicating
that text upto the end of the line is a comment is added.
Additional preprocessor directives introduced with "@" are
also added.
.!
.! ------------------------
.!
.sp 2
.need 6
These options are available,
.!
.openitem
.!
.item -l
Produce a
.filename _.lst
file describing the methods defined, messages that don't have
a known return type, class variables, and other useful information
that other tools can use to update class information files (_datasets).
Useful in combination with
.b1 -n
option if you're updating the local
.filename dataset
file.
.!
.item -n
Suppress generation of the output file (_ie, no output).
Useful when just gathering information about a class,
or to test if a file will translate successfully.
.!
.item2 -o output__file
Specify an output file.
Normally a ".c" is appended to the input base pathname.
.!
.item2 -i include__dir
Specify additional include directories to have
.program ${progname}
cpp
search.
Normally you'd use the environment variables.
.!
.item2 -p pre____proc____flags
Allows user to pass flags and such to
.program cpp.
Useful to send small/large data model settings that would normally
be defined by the compiler.
But since the compiler isn't invoking
.program cpp,
these symbols aren't known at this time.
This parameter may be repeated many times.
.!
.item -w#
Sets the warning level for the translator.
Level 0 silences the translator;
no progress messages or status messages about the file are displayed.
Level 2 lets the translator to say no class defined in file.
Level 1 is default.
.!
.item -Wall
Same as -w3.  Included for compatibility with other compilers.
.!
.item -za
Accept the input as-is.
The preprocess is not invoked.
Useful for debugging.
.!
.item -zk
Keep the temporary output file from
.program cpp.
Normally this file is deleted after
.program ${progname}
has run, whether there was an error in translation or not.
Useful for figuring out why
.program ${progname}
is complaining of an error.
.!
.item -zp
Pipe the output file to stdout instead of creating a
.filename _.c
file.
Since the output filename can't be specified, you must you
I/O redirection to store the output somewhere else.
.!
.item -zt
Instructs the translator to print verbosely its progress
through the source file.
Useful for debugging.
.!
.closeitem
.!
.! --------------------
.!
.sp 2
The following directives are available in the
.filename _.m
file.
There must be
.underline
no
whitespace between the at-sign (_@) and the directive.
.openitem
.!
.item2 @defs (_class_name)
Inserts the class instance variables into the text stream.
A trailing semicolon (_;) is inserted into the text.
Example, @defs(Sequence).
.!
.item @end
Offically mark the end of a class definition.
.!
.item2 @implementation class_name:superclass{vars}
Defines the class for this input file.
'superclass' is optional, as is the instance's variable declaration.
.!
.item2 @includes (_)
If a file without any class definitions is being translated,
then this directive must follow the files #include directives.
After the text is translated,
.program ${progname}
needs to include
.filename <objc/objc.h>
again.
The include statement is normally triggered off a class definition,
but if the file includes none, it must be done manually.
See below for example use.
.!
.item2 @selector (_method)
Substitutes the internal method selector code for the text name.
Currently, the implementation converts the code and compile time;
therefore, the method name must be a constant.
Example, @selector(at:put:).
.!
.closeitem
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Example
Here is an example of two files, one defining a new class,
and the other a main() routine that references the new class.
.sp
The file
.filename main.m
contains a reference to the class:
.!
.opencode
//  main.m     Demonstration main program.

#include  <objc/NewBytes.h>
@includes()
//  Since we don't define a class here,
//  @includes() trails our includes

main()
{
   id   nb;

   nb = [NewBytes str:"!dlrow olleH"];
   [nb reverse];
   printf( "%s\n", [nb str] );
   [nb free];                  //  <-- rather important
}  /* main */
.closecode
.!
The file
.filename newbytes.m
contains a class the extends the foundation class 
.bold
ByteArray
with one new method, reverse, which calls a library routine
to reverse a string in-place.
.!
.opencode
//  NewBytes    Demonstration class

#ifndef __NEWBYTES__H
#define  __NEWBYTES__H  1

#include  <objc/NewBytes.h>

@implementation  NewBytes : ByteArray

- reverse
{
   str_reverse( IV(self) );   // Access the string array
   return self;
}
@end
#endif
.closecode
.!
Although the new class is unknown to the system,
the superclass is known, so instance variables can be located.
To translate, compile, and link this sample, the following commands
(or at least similar ones) could be issued.
First an interface file is created declaring the instance variables
and return return type for 'reverse'.
Then the two
.filename _.m
files are translated and compiled.
The prelinker creates a file containing a list of classes needed
to run this application, and that list is compiled.
Finally the three object files are linked with the system classes
and the standard libraries.
.!
.opencode
>>> oct -w2 main.m
(OCT 1.85 Translator)
Note: no classes defined in this file.
>>> cc main.c
>>> oct newbytes.m
(OCT 1.85 Translator)
>>> cc newbytes.c
>>> prelink ww.c main.m newbytes.m
(prelink 1.04)
>>> cc ww.c
>>> ln -o main main.o newbytes.o ww.o -looc:include/ocbase -lc
>>> main
Hello world!
.closecode
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Environment Variables
.!
.sp
The following variables are referenced.
Defaults may be installed here by the user.
Temporary files are stored in TMPDIR if Unix like,
CCTMP for Amigas, or TMPDIR, TMP or TEMP directories under MS-DOS.
Translator options are read from OCTOPT, and
.program cpp
options are read from OCTCPP.
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Files
.save
.nj
.nf
C:cpp
C:oct
C:prelink
ooc:include/objc/Object.h
ooc:include/objc/objc.h
.restore
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section See Also
cc(CP), cpp(CP), ln(CP).
.br
prelink(OOPS)
.br
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Diagnostics
If the translator gets hung up on an expression it will usually abort.
A message of cause is printed, but it may not mean much.
The message "flex input buffer overflowed" may be related
to strings in the file.
Check the C output file for the last token the translator
was able to read.
.sp
.!
"method is generic" means that no description of a method selector was
found in the method interface definition files.
This warning isn't fatal; it just means that no type casting
of the return value will be performed.
The compiler will probably also complain if the return value
is assigned to a non-id type variable.
.sp
.!
If there is a type disagreement between any interface file
and a method call, either the warnings "mismatch return type"
or "mismatch parameter type" will be displayed.
Currently, the storeage class of each parameter must match;
this should be relaxed in the future.
.!
Fatal errors include unable to system()
.program cpp.
If the preprocessor returns an error value, then translation stops.
.!
.! +++++++++++++++++++++++++
.!
.section Credits
The preprocessor provided with the system is from DECUS, and was
ported to the Amiga by Fred Fish.
The program has been tweeked to accept "//" comments, and recogize
the #import directive.
This makes it incompatible with regular
AT&T UNIX
.program cpp
program, but more like GNU 
.program cpp.
.br
The ${product} system is based on Dr. Cox's Objective-C, version 2,
as described in his book,
.underline word
Object Oriented Programming: An Evolutionary Approach, 
.underline all
.underline off
Addison-Wesley, 1986.
Extensions are from versions 2 and 3 of
.program objc
from NeXT, which includes @abstract and some of the class methods.
The system was developed using
.program flex
and
.program bison
from the Free Software Foundation.
Only
.program flex
is used in the release version.
.sp
.!
Please abide by the software licences of
the Free Software Foundation
and DECUS
with their respective products.
DECUS is an independent user's group.
.sp
.!
The translator is about 120K of source; the classes are another
60K of code.
The system was developed on an Amiga 1000 with 1.5 Meg memory
using Manx C vers 3.6A.
Especially enjoyable are the Amiga's multi-tasking, windowing,
and dynamicly sizing RAM disk.
Stu Ferguson helped spot deficiencies in the whole system.
Thanks to my ex-manager Greg O'Feish for allowing me time to
bring this system to life.
.!
